opedia/frederick-iv-prince-of-salm-kyrburg"><span class="enc-name">Frederick IV, Prince of Salm-Kyrburg</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Frederick IV was the last reigning Prince of Salm-Kyrburg, a small German princely house of the Holy Roman Empire.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/frederick-lugard"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/frederick-lugard.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Frederick Lugard</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Frederick Lugard was a British colonial administrator who became high commissioner of the Protectorate of Northern Nigeria in 1900.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/frederick-middleton"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/frederick-middleton.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Frederick Middleton</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Sir Frederick Middleton was a British army officer who commanded the Canadian militia from 1884 to 1890.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/frederick-moss"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/frederick-moss.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Frederick Moss</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Frederick Moss was a New Zealand politician who served as British Resident in the Cook Islands from 1891 to 1898.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/frederick-peake"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/frederick-peake.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Frederick Peake</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Frederick Peake, known as Peake Pasha, was a British army officer who founded and commanded the Arab Legion, the army of the Emirate of Transjordan, from the early…</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/frederick-roberts-1st-earl-roberts"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/frederick-roberts-1st-earl-roberts.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Frederick Roberts, 1st Earl Roberts</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Frederick Roberts, 1st Earl Roberts, was a British field marshal.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/frederick-seymour"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/frederick-seymour.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Frederick Seymour</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Frederick Seymour was a British colonial official who succeeded James Douglas as governor of the mainland Colony of British Columbia in 1864.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/frederick-stanley-arnot"><span class="enc-name">Frederick Stanley Arnot</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Frederick Stanley Arnot (1858–1914) was a Scottish missionary of the Plymouth Brethren who travelled widely in south-central Africa.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/frederick-the-fair"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/frederick-the-fair.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Frederick the Fair</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Frederick the Fair was a Habsburg Duke of Austria and Styria from 1308 and German anti-king from 1314, elected by one faction against Louis IV of Bavaria.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/frederick-the-great"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/frederick-the-great.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Frederick the Great</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Frederick the Great, Frederick II of Prussia, was king from 1740.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/frederick-v-of-the-palatinate"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/frederick-v-of-the-palatinate.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Frederick V of the Palatinate</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Frederick V was Elector Palatine and the leading Calvinist prince of the Holy Roman Empire.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/frederick-v-landgrave-of-hesse-homburg"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/frederick-v-landgrave-of-hesse-homburg.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Frederick V, Landgrave of Hesse-Homburg</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Frederick V was Landgrave of Hesse-Homburg from 1751, ruling the small Hessian state from Bad Homburg.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/frederick-vi-of-denmark"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/frederick-vi-of-denmark.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Frederick VI of Denmark</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Frederick VI was King of Denmark and Norway from 1808 and regent from 1784, also ruling the duchies of Schleswig and Holstein.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/frederick-vii-of-denmark"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/frederick-vii-of-denmark.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Frederick VII of Denmark</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Frederick VII was King of Denmark from 1848 until his death in 1863, the last of the senior Oldenburg line.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/frederick-vii-count-of-toggenburg"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/frederick-vii-count-of-toggenburg.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Frederick VII, Count of Toggenburg</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Frederick VII was the last Count of Toggenburg, ruling a large territory in eastern Switzerland that stretched from the Toggenburg valley to the Prättigau and Davos.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/frederick-william-constantine-prince-of-hohenzollern-hechingen"><span class="enc-name">Frederick William Constantine, Prince of Hohenzollern-Hechingen</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Frederick William Constantine was the last Prince of Hohenzollern-Hechingen, reigning from 1838.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/frederick-william-i-of-prussia"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/frederick-william-i-of-prussia.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Frederick William I of Prussia</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Frederick William I was King in Prussia from 1713 to 1740, known as the Soldier King.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/frederick-william-i-duke-of-saxe-weimar"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/frederick-william-i-duke-of-saxe-weimar.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Frederick William I, Duke of Saxe-Weimar</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Frederick William I was a Saxon duke of the Ernestine line who received the lands around Weimar in the Erfurt Division of 1572.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/frederick-william-ii-of-prussia"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/frederick-william-ii-of-prussia.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Frederick William II of Prussia</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Frederick William II was King of Prussia from 1786 to 1797, nephew and successor of Frederick the Great.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/frederick-william-iv-of-prussia"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/frederick-william-iv-of-prussia.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Frederick William IV of Prussia</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Frederick William IV was king of Prussia from 1840 to 1861.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/frederick-william-koko"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/frederick-william-koko.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Frederick William Koko</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Frederick William Koko was king of Nembe, also called Brass, in the Niger delta of present-day Nigeria, from 1889.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/frederick-william-duke-of-brunswick-wolfenbuttel"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/frederick-william-duke-of-brunswick-wolfenbuttel.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Frederick William, Duke of Brunswick-Wolfenbüttel</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Frederick William, Duke of Brunswick-Wolfenbüttel, known as the Black Duke, lost his lands when Napoleon absorbed them into the Kingdom of Westphalia in 1807.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/frederick-william-duke-of-mecklenburg-schwerin"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/frederick-william-duke-of-mecklenburg-schwerin.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Frederick William, Duke of Mecklenburg-Schwerin</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Frederick William was Duke of Mecklenburg-Schwerin from 1692 to 1713.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/frederick-william-elector-of-brandenburg"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/frederick-william-elector-of-brandenburg.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Frederick William, Elector of Brandenburg</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Frederick William, Elector of Brandenburg, known as the Great Elector, ruled Brandenburg-Prussia from 1640.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/frederick-william-elector-of-hesse"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/frederick-william-elector-of-hesse.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Frederick William, Elector of Hesse</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Frederick William I was the last Elector of Hesse, co-regent from 1831 and sole ruler from 1847 to 1866.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/frederick-duke-of-saxe-altenburg"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/frederick-duke-of-saxe-altenburg.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Frederick, Duke of Saxe-Altenburg</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Frederick was Duke of Saxe-Hildburghausen from 1780 and, after the Ernestine lands were rearranged in 1826, the first Duke of the new Saxe-Altenburg.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/frederick-margrave-of-brandenburg-bayreuth"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/frederick-margrave-of-brandenburg-bayreuth.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Frederick, Margrave of Brandenburg-Bayreuth</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Frederick was Margrave of Brandenburg-Bayreuth from 1735 to 1763 and husband of Wilhelmine, sister of Frederick the Great.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/fredericton"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/fredericton.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Fredericton</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Fredericton is the capital of the Canadian province of New Brunswick, on the Saint John River.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/frederik-x"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/frederik-x.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Frederik X</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Frederik X is King of Denmark, who succeeded on 14 January 2024 when his mother, Queen Margrethe II, abdicated.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/free-aceh-movement"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/free-aceh-movement.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Free Aceh Movement</span><span class="enc-type">People / group</span><span class="enc-snip">The Free Aceh Movement, Gerakan Aceh Merdeka, was a separatist organisation founded in 1976 by Hasan di Tiro to win independence for Aceh from Indonesia.</span></a></li></ul>
